**B is <2-methylpropanoyl chloride>, $\mathrm{(CH_3)_2CHC(=O)Cl}$.** The single index of <index of hydrogen deficiency> is supplied by the <carbonyl>. Its relatively high-frequency <infrared spectroscopy> band identifies an <acyl chloride>, while two equivalent <methyl groups> explain why four <carbons> produce only three <carbon-13 NMR> resonances.

Assign $\delta_C=178.1$ to the <acyl chloride> <carbonyl>, $46.3$ to the methine and $18.9$ ppm to both <methyl groups>. Their <chemical equivalence in NMR> follows from the symmetry of the isopropyl fragment in this achiral environment.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The strong $1810\,\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ band is an <acyl chloride> <carbonyl stretching vibration>. Compared with an <ester>, donation from a <chlorine> <lone pair> into the <carbonyl> <antibonding molecular orbital> is relatively ineffective, while <chlorine>'s <inductive effect> withdraws <electron> density. The resulting stronger effective <carbonyl> force constant places the absorption above an ordinary saturated <ketone> band. Bond polarity alone is not a universal quantitative frequency rule.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The two <methyl groups> give one six-<proton> <NMR doublet> centred at $(1.319+1.296)/2=1.3075$ ppm. The methine gives a one-<proton> <NMR septet> centred near $2.976$ ppm. The latter is split by six equivalent methyl <protons>, producing the first-order $1:6:15:20:15:6:1$ intensity pattern.
$$
\boxed{{}^3J_{\mathrm{CH,CH_3}}\simeq6.9\,\mathrm{Hz}.}
$$
This follows exactly from the methyl doublet spacing, $300(0.023)$, and approximately from the successive septet spacings, which alternate between $0.022$ and $0.024$ ppm within the precision of the printed labels. There is no methylene triplet or quartet: the six-<proton> integral and septet establish the branched skeleton.

