$$
\boxed{pV=nRT.}
$$
Here $p$ is absolute <pressure>, $V$ is <volume (thermodynamics)>, $n$ is <amount of substance> in <moles>, $R$ is the <molar gas constant> and $T$ is absolute <thermodynamic temperature> in kelvin. This is the <ideal gas law>; consistent SI units give <pressure> in pascals, volume in cubic metres and $R$ in $\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <ideal gas> model takes the particles' own volume to be negligible compared with the container volume and neglects intermolecular interactions except during idealized elastic collisions. These assumptions remove excluded-volume and attractive-force corrections to the <ideal gas law>. A classical dilute gas also requires that quantum-statistical effects be negligible, but the first two are the requested mechanical assumptions.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The **<standard enthalpy of formation>** is the <enthalpy> change on forming exactly one <mole> of the specified compound in its stated <standard state> from its constituent <chemical elements> in their reference states at the same specified <temperature>. The reference states are normally the stable elemental forms at the standard <pressure>. A <standard state> does not itself mean 298 K; <temperature> must be stated separately.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The <Clausius statement of the second law> forbids a cyclic device whose sole effect is transfer of <heat> from a colder <thermal reservoir> to a hotter one. The <Kelvin-Planck statement of the second law> forbids a cyclic device whose sole effect is complete conversion of <heat> drawn from a single <thermal reservoir> into <work>. These are equivalent formulations of the <Second law of thermodynamics>; an ordinary refrigerator is allowed because supplied <work> is an additional effect.

= v
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

For $\mathrm{C_6H_6(l)\rightarrow6C(graphite)+3H_2(g)}$, reversing the standard formation reaction gives $\Delta_rG^\circ=-124.3\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Thus decomposition is thermodynamically favoured under the corresponding standard conditions. **<benzene> persists because it is kinetically stable on the laboratory timescale.** Breaking and reorganizing its bonding framework requires access to high-<activation energy> pathways, and suitable reactive intermediates or catalysts are absent under ordinary storage conditions. A negative <Gibbs free energy> change specifies the favoured thermodynamic direction, not a rapid <reaction rate>; thermal energy does not automatically overcome a large barrier.

= vi
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vi}

Combining $\Delta_rG^\circ=\Delta_rH^\circ-T\Delta_rS^\circ$ with the equilibrium relation gives
$$
\boxed{\ln K=-\frac{\Delta_rH^\circ}{R}\frac1T+\frac{\Delta_rS^\circ}{R}.}
$$
If <reaction enthalpy> and <reaction entropy> are approximately constant over the measurement interval, a plot of $y=\ln K$ against $x=1/T$ is straight, with **slope $-\Delta_rH^\circ/R$ and intercept $\Delta_rS^\circ/R$**. An exothermic reaction therefore has a positive slope. In the more general local form, the <Gibbs–Helmholtz equation> gives
$$
\frac{\mathrm d\ln K}{\mathrm d(1/T)}=-\frac{\Delta_rH^\circ(T)}R,
$$
which is the <Van't Hoff equation> without assuming a constant <enthalpy>.

Use a dimensionless <equilibrium constant> built from <thermodynamic activities>. Measure sufficiently separated temperatures after verifying true equilibrium, and avoid phase changes or a changing chemical reaction. Temperature-dependent <heat capacities> cause curvature: the fitted straight-line slope then estimates an interval-dependent <enthalpy>. Small errors in the equilibrium measurements can produce large slope errors over a narrow $1/T$ range. The <entropy> intercept is particularly sensitive because $1/T=0$ requires distant extrapolation, and slope/intercept errors are correlated. A numerical line through a few points is not, by itself, evidence that all these assumptions hold.

\Image[paper-1-vanthoff-plot.png]
{title=Original schematic van't Hoff plot showing how slope and intercept encode enthalpy and entropy}

= vii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vii}

The <exponential function> makes modest <Gibbs free energy> differences correspond to extreme equilibrium ratios. At 298 K, $RT\simeq2.48\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$; for example,
$$
\Delta_rG^\circ=+100\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}\Rightarrow K\simeq3.0\times10^{-18},\qquad
\Delta_rG^\circ=-100\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}\Rightarrow K\simeq3.4\times10^{17}.
$$
The minor equilibrium component may therefore lie below detection, or a species' required <thermodynamic activity> may be experimentally inaccessible. Apparently complete conversion supplies a bound on $K$, rather than an accurate finite value. Additionally, some reactions do not reach <chemical equilibrium> within a practicable observation <time> because of kinetic barriers. The thermodynamic identity still holds even when direct room-temperature determination of both sides of the equilibrium ratio fails.

