= Solution
{parent=i}

For red-to-yellow conversion, subtract the red values from the yellow values:
$$
\Delta_rH^\circ=(-90.2)-(-90.7)=+0.5\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}},\qquad
\Delta_rS^\circ=73.0-72.0=+1.0\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}\,mol^{-1}}.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{\Delta_rG^\circ(T)=500-T\quad\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}},\qquad\Delta_rG^\circ(298\,\mathrm K)=+202\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}\simeq+0.20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}.}
$$
The positive value makes the red form the lower-<Gibbs free energy> form at 298 K within the supplied bulk-phase model. The calculation uses 298 K as the conventional reference <temperature>; the paper supplies no separate numerical <temperature> for this subpart. Keeping <enthalpy> and <entropy> in the same energy units is essential.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At coexistence of the two pure solid forms, their <chemical potentials> are equal and $\Delta_rG^\circ=0$. Treating the tabulated <enthalpy> and <entropy> differences as constant gives
$$
\boxed{T_{\rm tr}=\frac{\Delta_rH^\circ}{\Delta_rS^\circ}=\frac{500}{1.0}=500\,\mathrm{K}\simeq227\,{}^\circ\mathrm{C}.}
$$
Above this <temperature> the model favours the higher-entropy yellow form; below it the red form is favoured. This is a prediction from the given thermodynamic data, not a guarantee of an immediately visible colour change: kinetic trapping, particle-size effects and temperature-dependent <heat> capacities are not included. A ratio of masses of two coexisting pure solids is not the <equilibrium constant>, since their bulk solid activities are each unity.

