= Solution
{parent=i}

With the forward direction defined as cis-to-trans, the elementary steps have rates $k_1[\mathrm{cis}]$ and $k_{-1}[\mathrm{trans}]$. One trans molecule is gained per forward event and lost per reverse event, so
$$
\boxed{\frac{\mathrm d[\mathrm{trans}]}{\mathrm dt}=k_1[\mathrm{cis}]-k_{-1}[\mathrm{trans}].}
$$
Each <rate constant> has units $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, and the right side has units of <concentration> per unit <time>. This net rate can have either sign, even though the individual event rates are nonnegative.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At <chemical equilibrium>, the forward and reverse event rates are equal, rather than both reactions stopping:
$$
k_1[\mathrm{cis}]_{\rm eq}=k_{-1}[\mathrm{trans}]_{\rm eq}.
$$
For the dilute-solution <concentration> model,
$$
\boxed{K_{\rm eq}=\frac{[\mathrm{trans}]_{\rm eq}}{[\mathrm{cis}]_{\rm eq}}=\frac{k_1}{k_{-1}}.}
$$
The ratio is dimensionless because both <concentrations> and both <rate constants> have matching units. An exact thermodynamic <equilibrium constant> uses activities; here the common standard <concentration> and ideal-solution activity coefficients cancel.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let $C_{\rm tot}=[\mathrm{cis}]+[\mathrm{trans}]$, which is conserved for this closed one-to-one <isomerization> at fixed volume. Substitute $[\mathrm{cis}]_{298}=C_{\rm tot}-[\mathrm{trans}]_{298}$ into the equilibrium ratio:
$$
[\mathrm{trans}]_{298}=K_{\rm eq}(C_{\rm tot}-[\mathrm{trans}]_{298}).
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{[\mathrm{trans}]_{298}=\frac{K_{\rm eq}}{1+K_{\rm eq}}C_{\rm tot},\qquad[\mathrm{cis}]_{298}=\frac{1}{1+K_{\rm eq}}C_{\rm tot}.}
$$
The equilibrium split is independent of having started with pure cis, provided equilibration is complete and no side reaction changes the total <concentration>.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The forward and reverse pathways pass through the same <transition state>. Measured from the cis minimum, the forward barrier is $E_{a,1}$; measured from the trans minimum, the reverse barrier is $E_{a,-1}$. Since $E_{a,-1}=E_{a,1}+2.23\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, the trans minimum lies **$2.23\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ below the cis minimum** in the elementary energy-profile model:
$$
E_{\rm trans}-E_{\rm cis}=E_{a,1}-E_{a,-1}=-2.23\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}.
$$
The absolute barrier heights cannot be deduced from their difference. The following original profile uses an arbitrary transition-state height and labels that distinction explicitly.

\Image[paper-1-isomerization-energy.png]
{title=Original elementary energy profile with trans below cis and the reverse barrier larger by 2.23 kJ per mole}

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The equal <Arrhenius prefactors> cancel in the ratio:
$$
K_{298}=\frac{Ae^{-E_{a,1}/RT}}{Ae^{-E_{a,-1}/RT}}
=\exp\!\left(\frac{E_{a,-1}-E_{a,1}}{RT}\right)
=\exp\!\left(\frac{2230}{8.3145\times298}\right).
$$
Hence **$K_{298}=2.46$**, favouring trans, with trans fraction $K/(1+K)=0.71095$. The factor 1000 converting the barrier difference from kilojoules to joules is essential.

