At the higher <temperature> the same equilibrium rate balance gives
$$
\boxed{k'_{-1}=\frac{k'_1}{K_{318}}=\frac{1.04\times10^{-3}}{2.31}=4.50\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}.}
$$
This is a reverse <first-order rate constant>, not the net relaxation rate, which includes both elementary directions.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**The <temperature> increase shifts the equilibrium towards cis.** The ratio $[\mathrm{trans}]/[\mathrm{cis}]$ decreases from about $2.46$ to $2.31$, so the trans fraction falls from $0.71095$ to $2.31/3.31=0.69789$. Trans remains the more abundant isomer; “favours cis” describes the direction of the change, rather than requiring cis to become the majority.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Integrating the <Van't Hoff equation> with approximately constant <reaction enthalpy> gives
$$
\ln\!\left(\frac{K_{318}}{K_{298}}\right)=-\frac{\Delta_rH^\circ}{R}\left(\frac1{318}-\frac1{298}\right).
$$
Using the full calculated $K_{298}=2.4596538\ldots$ yields
$$
\boxed{\Delta_rH^\circ=-8.3145\frac{\ln(2.31/2.4596538\ldots)}{1/318-1/298}=-2.47\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}.}
$$
The negative sign agrees with the decrease of $K$ on heating. This is the two-temperature estimate from the supplied equilibrium data. It is not exactly the $-2.23\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ implied by treating equal prefactors and a fixed activation-energy difference as exact throughout the interval: that stricter model predicts $K_{318}=2.3243$ rather than $2.31$. The data and idealizations are therefore only approximately mutually consistent; no rounding of intermediate calculations can make both numerical <enthalpy> estimates identical.

= d
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Eliminate cis using $[\mathrm{cis}]=C_{\rm tot}-[\mathrm{trans}]$:
$$
\frac{\mathrm d[\mathrm{trans}]}{\mathrm dt}
=k'_1C_{\rm tot}-(k'_1+k'_{-1})[\mathrm{trans}].
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{c_1=k'_1=1.04\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}},\qquad c_2=k'_1+k'_{-1}=1.4902\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}.}
$$
Both directions restore the perturbed distribution: an excess of trans both increases its reverse consumption and reduces cis available for forward formation. This explains why the <chemical relaxation time> is $1/(k'_1+k'_{-1})$, rather than the reciprocal of only one <rate constant>.

= e
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Take the rapid <temperature jump> to occur at $t=0$. Neglect thermal expansion as in the stated constant-$C_{\rm tot}$ model. The composition is initially unchanged, so the curve is continuous through $t=0$, with
$$
\frac{[\mathrm{trans}]_{\rm eq1}}{C_{\rm tot}}=0.71095,\qquad
\frac{[\mathrm{trans}]_{\rm eq2}}{C_{\rm tot}}=0.69789.
$$
It is horizontal at the first value before the jump, then **decreases exponentially towards the lower second plateau**, without overshoot:
$$
\frac{[\mathrm{trans}](t)}{C_{\rm tot}}=0.69789+0.01307\,e^{-0.0014902t}\quad(t\ge0).
$$
The initial post-jump slope is negative, and the curve is concave upward. A discontinuous vertical step in the composition would incorrectly make a finite reaction happen instantaneously.

\Image[paper-1-isomerization-relaxation.png]
{title=Original normalized trans-concentration relaxation curve, continuous at the temperature jump and approaching its new lower plateau}

= ii
{parent=e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Subtract the new equilibrium <concentration> and divide by the initial displacement:
$$
\frac{[\mathrm{trans}](t)-[\mathrm{trans}]_{\rm eq2}}{[\mathrm{trans}]_{\rm eq1}-[\mathrm{trans}]_{\rm eq2}}
=e^{-(k'_1+k'_{-1})t}.
$$
At the halfway <concentration> this ratio is $1/2$, so
$$
\boxed{t_{1/2}=\frac{\ln2}{k'_1+k'_{-1}}=465\,\mathrm s\simeq7.75\,\mathrm{min}.}
$$
This is the half-time of the displacement from equilibrium. It is not the <time> for the absolute trans <concentration> to fall to half its initial value; that value lies far below the attainable new equilibrium plateau. The corresponding <chemical relaxation time> is $671$ s.

