= Solution
{parent=i}

In molecular <boron trichloride>, <boron> uses three approximately $sp^2$ hybrids to form three equivalent B–Cl <sigma bonds> in a trigonal plane. Its perpendicular empty $2p$ orbital can accept <electron> density from the compatible combination of filled <chlorine> $3p$ <lone pairs>. The resulting <pi donation> is delocalized over the three B–Cl connections.

In the elementary model, one net <pi bond> is distributed over three equivalent <sigma bonds>, giving **formal average B–Cl <bond order> $1+1/3=4/3$**. The corresponding <resonance contributors> each put a B=Cl connection in a different position, with formal negative <boron> and positive donating <chlorine>. They do not mean one experimentally distinct double bond and two single bonds. The $4/3$ value is the idealized sigma-plus-delocalized-pi bookkeeping value; a computed bond index need not equal that exact rational number.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The dimer has four terminal chlorides and two <bridging chloride ligands>. Each <aluminium> is approximately tetrahedrally surrounded by two terminal and two bridging chlorines. A <chlorine> <lone pair> from one $\mathrm{AlCl_3}$ fragment donates into an empty orbital of the other <Lewis acid> centre; reciprocal donation closes the four-membered $\mathrm{Al_2Cl_2}$ ring.

The localized <coordinate covalent bond> model has **single Al–Cl bonds, formal <bond order> one, for both terminal and bridging connections**. Donation describes the origin of a pair, not half an electron-pair bond. A bridge <chlorine> has two bonded pairs and two remaining <lone pairs> in this all-single-bond Lewis accounting; the original terminal chlorines have three <lone pairs>. Bridging contacts are longer and weaker than terminal ones, so equal formal order does not imply equal strength or equal numerical quantum-chemical bond indices. In particular, these <chloride> bridges should not be assigned the electron-deficient methyl-bridge half-bonds of (b).

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Dimerization lowers <enthalpy> by letting the coordinatively unsaturated <aluminium> centres accept <chlorine> lone-pair donation. Effective intramolecular Cl-to-Al pi stabilization of the monomer is relatively weak, while <aluminium>'s larger size accommodates four-coordinate geometry and two <chloride> bridges. At room <temperature> this bonding gain can outweigh the <entropy> penalty of associating two molecules.

In <boron trichloride>, the compact short B–Cl framework supports appreciable ligand-to-boron <pi donation>, stabilizing the planar monomer. Its smaller <boron> centre also makes the crowded four-coordinate bridged arrangement less favourable. Thus dimerization must be assessed from the balance of monomer stabilization, bridge-bond formation, structural strain and <entropy>; merely counting six electrons around each monomeric centre would incorrectly predict identical association for both. <orbital overlap> depends on radial extent, bond length and energy matching, not just whether two orbitals share the same principal quantum number.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**Heating increases the monomer fraction through $\mathrm{Al_2Cl_6(g)\rightleftharpoons2AlCl_3(g)}$.** Dissociation breaks the bridge interactions and gains translational <entropy> by making two gas molecules from one. Its <enthalpy> cost becomes easier to offset at higher <temperature>. The qualitative statement concerns the molecular gas-phase equilibrium at the stated conditions, rather than every solid or liquid phase of <aluminium chloride>.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The four terminal <methyl groups> form ordinary Al–C <sigma bonds>, with formal <bond order> one. Each of the two bridging methyl carbons connects the two <aluminium> centres through a <three-centre two-electron bond>. One occupied bonding combination extends over Al–C–Al; the other combinations are unoccupied in the simple electron-deficient bridge model. A single shared pair therefore supplies one bond in total across the two contacts, giving **formal Al–C bridge <bond order> $1/2$ per contact**, compared with one for terminal Al–C.

Each <aluminium> has approximately tetrahedral, $sp^3$-like orbital directions. A terminal methyl <carbon> uses $sp^3$-like orbitals for its three C–H bonds and its Al–C bond. A bridging methyl <carbon> still uses three directed orbitals for C–H bonding, while its remaining approximately $sp^3$ hybrid overlaps with orbitals on both <aluminium> atoms. This shared bridge avoids placing five independent ordinary two-electron bonds around <carbon>. The hybrid labels are idealized and the bridge geometry is distorted; the four occupied C–H/bridge orbital directions are not equivalent to five localized <carbon> bonds.

The total valence count provides a useful check: $2(3)+6(4)+18(1)=48$ electrons. Eighteen C–H bonds use 36, four terminal Al–C bonds use eight, and two three-centre bridges use four. A description assigning a full two-electron bond to each of the four bridging contacts would require too many electrons.

\Image[paper-1-aluminium-bridges.png]
{title=Original comparison of chloride lone-pair bridges and electron-deficient methyl bridges, with their different electron accounting}

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At low <temperature> the four terminal <methyl groups> contain 12 equivalent protons and the two bridging methyls contain six, giving the **$2:1$ pair of singlets**. On warming, terminal and bridging positions interchange rapidly on the <NMR timescale>. A <methyl group> then samples both environments during the observation, producing a single population-weighted resonance, $\delta_{\rm av}=(2\delta_{\rm term}+\delta_{\rm bridge})/3$. This <Chemical exchange in NMR> is not merely faster rotation of each methyl about its own Al–C direction, which cannot turn a terminal site into a bridge.

The <concentration> independence argues against a rate-limiting collision between two intact dimers. An intramolecular bridge-opening and reclosing rearrangement can exchange which methyls are bridges; a unimolecular dissociation followed by rapid reassociation is another mechanism whose observed rate could be <concentration> independent. **The spectra establish rapid site exchange, but not a unique elementary exchange pathway.** A variable-temperature line-shape analysis or exchange experiment would be needed to distinguish detailed mechanisms. Primary organoaluminium studies describe the same low-temperature resolution and warm-temperature averaging, while considering dimer dissociation.<Organoaluminium compounds. XIII. Association through aryl and phenylethynyl bridges by E. A. Jeffery and others>[1968, printed p. 137, introduction: separate methyl environments and temperature-induced averaging]{ref}

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The simplest assignment is **two phenylethynyl bridges and four equivalent terminal <methyl groups>**, represented as $\mathrm{Al_2Me_4(\mu-C\equiv CPh)_2}$. All 12 methyl protons then occupy the same class of environment, accounting for the single nonaromatic singlet without requiring methyl bridge/terminal averaging. The phenyl protons account for the aromatic-region signals; the metal-bound acetylide has no terminal acetylenic <hydrogen>.

An <alkynyl bridge> has electronic possibilities unavailable to a saturated methyl: in addition to sigma attachment from its <carbon> end to one <Lewis acid> centre, its occupied C≡C pi system can donate to the other <aluminium>. A delocalized sigma/pi description stabilizes the phenylethynyl-bridged arrangement while keeping the <methyl groups> terminal. One must not draw an unchanged localized triple bond plus two independent full ordinary Al–C bonds at the same <carbon> and count all of them as separate two-electron bonds. The exact distribution of sigma and pi donation requires structural or orbital evidence; this NMR observation alone does not supply a numerical bridge <bond order>.

The absence of <concentration> or <temperature> dependence is consistent with this preferred arrangement throughout the measured range. It is not proof that the molecule has no motion: exchange between symmetry-equivalent terminal positions, or exchange remaining fast throughout that range, could be spectroscopically invisible. Phenylethynyl bridging in this particular dimethylaluminium dimer has independent primary NMR precedent.<Organoaluminium compounds. XIII. Association through aryl and phenylethynyl bridges by E. A. Jeffery and others>[1968, printed p. 137, summary: phenylethynyl groups bridge dimethyl(phenylethynyl)aluminium dimers]{ref}
